title: Mount point detection
tags: [[Mount points]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: A directory is a mount point iff the kernel's mount table says so (cached one second); without /proc, a device id differing from the parent's — with two known blind spots.

''Linux — ask the kernel.'' A path is a mount point iff it appears as one in
`/proc/self/mountinfo`, authoritative for the daemon's mount namespace, and the only
source that gets both of these right:

* a `mount --bind` of a directory of the ''same'' filesystem shares its superblock, so its device id equals its parent's — invisible to a device comparison, yet `umount` still empties it (every real filesystem, FUSE ones included, has its own superblock, so this sharing is the comparison's only blind spot);
* btrfs gives each ''subvolume'' an anonymous device id, so a device comparison calls a plain subvolume boundary a mount point, though nothing can be unplugged there.

The table is re-read at most once per second, as a process-wide snapshot: every directory
a reconcile touches asks, and the kernel regenerates the file on each read. A second of
staleness is harmless — mounting triggers nothing by itself. Paths are matched verbatim
against the table's canonical form: every path the daemon builds starts at the
canonicalised root and never crosses a symlinked directory, so no per-probe
`canonicalize` is needed.

''Other Unix (macOS), or Linux without a readable `/proc`'': compare the directory's
device id with its parent's (`st_dev`), with the two blind spots above. An empty table
snapshot is the signal to fall back.

The repository root is never marked: its parent lies outside the repository.

''Windows'': no device id is exposed, and a removable volume is usually a drive letter —
its own repository root. Mount points are not detected there: `mfr_mount` is never
written. A repository whose fields were written on Unix keeps them, and a Windows
daemon still honours the freeze, which needs only the field and an "is something
mounted here" answer.

`crates/daemon/src/mount.rs`; `stat_fields_in` adds `mfr_mount` on a live mount point.
